dtd: keep the GPU copies coherent with the CPU tasks of parsec_dtd_insert_task

A task class created by parsec_dtd_insert_task (fpointer API) calls the user
function directly as the CPU hook, while the CPU chores added by
parsec_dtd_task_class_add_chore go through parsec_dtd_cpu_task_submit. That
wrapper increments the version of the written host copies and takes the
ownership back from a GPU. Without it, a CPU task of the fpointer API that
writes a tile last written on a GPU leaves the GPU copy owner, with the same
version as the host copy: the next GPU task that reads the tile uses the stale
GPU copy, without any transfer.

This happens as soon as the fpointer API and task classes with a GPU chore are
mixed on the same data (Chameleon: getrf_nopiv on the CPU between trsm/gemm on
the GPU gives wrong factorizations). Use the wrapper for both APIs.

diff --git a/parsec/interfaces/dtd/insert_function.c b/parsec/interfaces/dtd/insert_function.c
index 2ac4fef34..25cfd8eac 100644
--- a/parsec/interfaces/dtd/insert_function.c
+++ b/parsec/interfaces/dtd/insert_function.c
@@ -3469,8 +3469,11 @@ __parsec_dtd_taskpool_create_task(parsec_taskpool_t *tp,
                 dtd_tc->gpu_func_ptr = (parsec_advance_task_function_t)fpointer;
             }
             else {
-                /* Default case: the user-provided function is directly the hook to call */
-                incarnations[0].hook = fpointer; // We can directly call the CPU hook
+                /* Go through the CPU submit wrapper, as the chores of
+                 * parsec_dtd_task_class_add_chore do: it moves the ownership
+                 * of the written data back to the host, so that the GPU copies
+                 * are not reused once the CPU modified the data. */
+                incarnations[0].hook = parsec_dtd_cpu_task_submit;
                 dtd_tc->cpu_func_ptr = fpointer;
             }
             incarnations[1].type = PARSEC_DEV_NONE;
